---
import CourseLayout from '../layouts/CourseLayout.astro';
---
<CourseLayout title="Team workshop · User Story Mapping" description="Two story mapping exercises to run with your own team: a ten-minute warm-up and a thirty-minute first map of a real journey." section="workshop">
  <main class="shell workshop" id="main">
    <a href="/" class="back-link print-hide">← Course overview</a>
    <header class="workshop-head">
      <p class="kicker short-rule">User story mapping</p>
      <h1>Team <em>workshop.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">Reading about a map teaches less than building one. These two exercises take a team from nothing to a first sliced map in forty minutes. You need sticky notes in two colours, pens and a clear wall or table.</p>
      <button class="secondary print-hide" data-print hidden>Print both exercises <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button>
    </header>

    <section class="facilitation" aria-labelledby="warm-up-title">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker short-rule">Exercise 1 · 10 minutes</p>
        <h2 id="warm-up-title">Map your morning</h2>
        <p>This is Patton’s own way of teaching the basics, from chapter 5 of the book. Everyone already knows the subject, so the group can concentrate on the method.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="agenda">
        <li><span>3 min</span><div><h3>Write your tasks</h3><p>Working alone, each person writes what they did this morning between waking and starting work. Use one note for each task, and start each with a verb.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>3 min</span><div><h3>Merge into one flow</h3><p>Combine everyone’s notes in a single row from left to right, in the order they happened. Stack near-duplicates. Where the order is argued over, it probably does not matter.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>2 min</span><div><h3>Name the activities</h3><p>Find the clusters, such as “get cleaned up” or “make breakfast”. Put a note of the other colour above each one. Read the backbone aloud.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>2 min</span><div><h3>Slice it</h3><p>You have overslept and must leave in ten minutes. Move every task you would skip below a line. What is left is the smallest morning that still gets you out of the door.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="facilitation" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker short-rule">Exercise 2 · 30 minutes</p>
        <h2 id="team-title">Map a real journey</h2>
        <p>Pick a feature or product your team is working on now. Make sure someone in the room has watched a real user go through it, or has done it themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="agenda">
        <li><span>4 min</span><div><h3>Frame it</h3><p>Write on one note who this is for, what they do today and what should change for them. Agree how you would know that it had changed. Stick the note at the top left.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>8 min</span><div><h3>Tell the story and build the backbone</h3><p>One person tells the user’s journey from the first cue to the moment they are done. Everyone else writes one task per note. Lay the tasks from left to right, then name the activities above them.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>6 min</span><div><h3>Play What-About</h3><p>Go along the map one column at a time. What about another kind of user? Another way of doing it? What goes wrong here? Put each answer beneath its task.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>6 min</span><div><h3>Slice a first release</h3><p>Reread the outcome from the framing note. Move everything it does not need below a line. Have someone who has not spoken much tell the story of what remains, from end to end.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>4 min</span><div><h3>Name the riskiest assumption</h3><p>Ask what must be true for that slice to work. Circle the assumption that is least certain and would hurt most. Agree the cheapest test and who will run it.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>2 min</span><div><h3>Keep it</h3><p>Photograph the map and record a one-minute video of someone talking it through. Leave the map on the wall and agree when you will next stand in front of it.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <p class="print-hide"><a class="text-link" href="/cheat-sheet/">Open the cheat sheet →</a></p>
  </main>
</CourseLayout>
<script>
  const print = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-print]');
  if (print) {
    print.hidden = false;
    print.addEventListener('click', () => window.print());
  }
</script>
